##2.2 Pooled OLS baseline

#a)
m1 = lm(Eval ~ Apct + Enrollment + Required, data = df)
summary(m1)
#The coefficient on Apct is 0.359, which means that a one-unit increase in Apct 
#is associated with a 0.359 point increase in the average teaching evaluation.

#b)
#1.Instructors who explain material clearly and engage students effectively may 
#receive higher evaluations and have more students performing well.

#2.Some instructors may design easier courses or grade more nicely, which could 
#increase both the share of A grades and students satisfaction with the course.

#Because these unobserved characteristics are likely positively correlated with Apct, 
# estimate is probably biased upward.

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
##2.3 Fixed effects models

#a)
m_instr = feols(Eval ~ Apct + Enrollment + Required | InstrID, data = df)
summary(m_instr)
m_twfe = feols(Eval ~ Apct + Enrollment + Required | InstrID + Year, data = df)
summary(m_twfe)

#b)
modelsummary(
  list("Pooled OLS" = m1, "Instructor FE" = m_instr, "Two-Way FE" = m_twfe),
  vcov = ~InstrID,
  stars = TRUE,
  gof_map = c("r.squared", "nobs"))

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
##2.4 Random effects and the Hausman test

#a)
pdata = pdata.frame(df, index = c("InstrID", "CourseID"))
m_re = plm(Eval ~ Apct + Enrollment + Required,
           data = pdata, model = "random")

#b)
m_fe_plm = plm(Eval ~ Apct + Enrollment + Required,
               data = pdata, model = "within")
phtest(m_fe_plm, m_re)

#c)
#The null hypothesis of the Hausman test is that the unobserved instructor-level 
#characteristics are not correlated with the regressors.The null hypothesis is not rejected,
#as the p-value is above 0,05. This suggests that the random effects assumption may be reasonable 
#and that the random effects estimator is consistent. However, based on the substantive reasoning 
#from the previous section it is still plausible that instructor characteristics such as teaching 
#ability or grading style affect both grading and evaluation scores. Overall, the fixed effects model
#is probably better as it more robustly controls for unobserved instructor characteristics.




